WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.423
The key idea is to introduce a trusted reference
classifier that knows the true semantics.

00:00:05.503 --> 00:00:10.927
On Contextual Stochastic Block Models the authors
can derive the Bayes optimal classifier,

00:00:11.007 --> 00:00:14.046
which chooses the most likely class given the data,

00:00:14.126 --> 00:00:16.675
and use it as the ground-truth reference g.

00:00:16.755 --> 00:00:23.279
An over-robust example is then a perturbed graph
where the model and the reference agree on the clean input,

00:00:23.359 --> 00:00:27.804
the perturbation flips the reference (so the
semantics genuinely changed),

00:00:27.884 --> 00:00:30.862
but the model stubbornly keeps its old prediction.

00:00:30.942 --> 00:00:37.527
They measure over-robustness as one minus the ratio
of semantics-aware robustness to conventional robustness,

00:00:37.607 --> 00:00:43.948
so a value of zero-point-two means twenty percent
of the measured robustness lies beyond semantic change.

00:00:44.028 --> 00:00:48.045
Finally, they combine graph neural networks with
label propagation,

00:00:48.125 --> 00:00:54.710
feeding the training graph's known labels into the
inference process to reduce this unwanted over-robustness.
